{"acceptedResponses":["E"],"answer":"E","bank":"amc","batch":"NA","choiceOrder":["A","B","C","D","E"],"choices":{"A":"undetermined","B":"\\(5\\)","C":"\\(6\\)","D":"\\(-13\\)","E":"\\(26\\)"},"choicesType":"text","domain":[],"editorialNote":"","figureSources":{},"id":"1962_AMC_12_Q21","knowledgeGraph":{"assessedIds":["cnm_h099"],"contentHash":"34ba138a2742d613f7683c59aa4e2241f6d2b28defdeb2b82d8716faa548e728","flags":[],"reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"substitute-complex-root","label":"Compare real and imaginary parts","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-complex-root":["cnm_h102","cnm_h099"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h102","cnm_h099"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"conjugate-root-product","label":"Use the conjugate root product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"conjugate-root-product":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h104"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h104"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_039","cnm_042"],"id":"compare-complex-parts","label":"Substitute the root and compare parts","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-complex-parts":["cnm_h099","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h099","cnm_h102","cnm_h097","cnm_039","cnm_042"]}],"status":"mapped","supportIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h102"],"version":"1.0.0"},"level":"AMC 12","number":21,"question":"It is given that one root of \\(2x^2+rx+s=0,\\) with \\(r\\) and \\(s\\) real numbers, is \\(3+2i\\) \\((i=\\sqrt{-1}).\\) The value of \\(s\\) is:","questionType":"text","rejectedAnswers":[],"responseType":"choice","schemaVersion":"3.0","skill":[],"solution":"Because \\(3+2i\\) is a root, substituting it makes the polynomial zero. This gives an equality whose real and imaginary parts must each be zero. Using \\(i^2=-1\\), first calculate\n\\[\n(3+2i)^2=9+12i+4i^2=5+12i.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=2(5+12i)+r(3+2i)+s\\\\\n&=(10+3r+s)+(24+2r)i.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(r\\) and \\(s\\) are real, the imaginary part gives \\(24+2r=0\\), so \\(r=-12\\). The real part then gives\n\\[\n10+3(-12)+s=0,\n\\]\nso \\(s=26\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).","solutionAvailability":"present","solutionMethods":[{"directNodeIds":["cnm_h099","cnm_h102"],"explanation":"Because \\(3+2i\\) is a root, substituting it makes the polynomial zero. This gives an equality whose real and imaginary parts must each be zero. Using \\(i^2=-1\\), first calculate\n\\[\n(3+2i)^2=9+12i+4i^2=5+12i.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=2(5+12i)+r(3+2i)+s\\\\\n&=(10+3r+s)+(24+2r)i.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(r\\) and \\(s\\) are real, the imaginary part gives \\(24+2r=0\\), so \\(r=-12\\). The real part then gives\n\\[\n10+3(-12)+s=0,\n\\]\nso \\(s=26\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).","id":"compare-complex-parts","label":"Substitute the root and compare parts","legacyRouteId":"compare-complex-parts","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Accepted primary worked-solution review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/1962_AMC_12_Q21.json","sha256":"b1dff677e65f148011b8fbea0cdd626fbd2207cd379514f8c39130e1c555c27b"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"AI-authored and independently AI-reviewed; not teacher reviewed","source":"Original Math Practice solution","sourceRef":"amc/1962_AMC_12_Q21#compare-complex-parts","sourceURL":"https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice"}},{"directNodeIds":["cnm_h102","cnm_h099"],"id":"substitute-complex-root","label":"Compare real and imaginary parts","legacyRouteId":"substitute-complex-root","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Legacy graph and grading review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/1962_AMC_12_Q21.json","sha256":"b1dff677e65f148011b8fbea0cdd626fbd2207cd379514f8c39130e1c555c27b"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","source":"Existing reviewed concept route","sourceRef":"amc/1962